When it measures the min-content block size of a replaced box whose
percentage height has no containing-block height to resolve against,
Ladybird treats that height as auto, and applies the cyclic-percentage
“resolve against zero” rule only in the inline axis.

Blink does the same. ResolveBlockLengthInternal() in length_utils.cc
falls back to the content block size for a percentage with an indefinite
basis, and only ComputeMinAndMaxContentContributionForReplaced() applies
the zero rule — to widths alone.

WebKit’s RenderReplaced::hasReplacedLogicalHeight() treats such a
percentage as auto. Its min-content zeroing is only in RenderReplaced::
computeIntrinsicLogicalWidthContributions(), and only for widths.

Gecko’s nsLayoutUtils::IsAutoBSize() treats it as auto too, and
nsLayoutUtils::IntrinsicForAxis() applies the zero rule only in the
inline axis. Its single block-axis use, in nsFlexContainerFrame::
PartiallyResolveAutoMinSize(), is for a replaced flex or grid item
itself, so it doesn’t apply to an image inside a flex item.
